WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.418
Alaska Airlines is

00:00:01.418 --> 00:00:02.377
now grounding

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.629
its entire fleet of Boeing 737

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:06.006
nine max planes

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.174
after a terrifying trip on Friday.

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.134
Alaska Airlines

00:00:09.134 --> 00:00:11.803
Flight 1282 had just taken off

00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:12.929
from Portland, Oregon,

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:15.682
when a section of its cabin ripped off,

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:16.850
leaving a gaping hole,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.393
as you see right there in the jet

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:20.937
with more than 170 passengers on board.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:21.855
One passenger

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:24.315
captured the scene during the very tense

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:25.608
but silent moments

00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:37.328
after the incident happened.

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:39.372
They are all nearly paralyzed in fear.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:42.042
Their plane was only airborne

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.209
for about 20 minutes

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.836
before the pilot made an emergency

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:46.254
landing back in Portland.

00:00:46.254 --> 00:00:47.922
One person was taken to the hospital

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:49.257
with a minor injury.

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:50.925
Another passenger describes

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:54.220
the harrowing ordeal.

00:00:54.304 --> 00:00:56.347
I understand talking to a lady

00:00:56.347 --> 00:00:57.724
who was sitting in the row

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:58.558
immediately behind

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.852
where the panel blew up, that there was a

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:01.936
you could see later

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:03.188
that there was a two window

00:01:03.188 --> 00:01:04.481
section panel that blew out.

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:05.982
It's about as wide as a refrigerator

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:07.776
and about two thirds as high.

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:09.319
And she said there was,

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:10.278
I guess a boy

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:12.489
and his mother were sitting in that row

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:13.782
and his shirt was sucked

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:15.366
off him and out of the plane

00:01:15.366 --> 00:01:18.161
and his mother was holding on to him.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:19.120
And she said her own little

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.123
boy's phone went out to

00:01:22.207 --> 00:01:23.291
incredible.

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:24.125
CNN aviation

00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:25.752
correspondent Pete Martin is tracking

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.337
all the latest developments.

00:01:27.337 --> 00:01:28.838
Pete, I mean, what do we know

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:30.381
about how this happened?

00:01:30.381 --> 00:01:32.258
This happened in a really explosive

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.051
moment, Fredricka.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:34.552
Like the most violent convertible rod

00:01:34.552 --> 00:01:36.137
you've ever been on, in this case,

00:01:36.137 --> 00:01:39.349
a Boeing 737 max, nine in row 26,

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.017
where there could have been a door.

00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:42.310
This plane did not have it

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.895
known as a door plug.

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:45.021
More on that in a second.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:46.481
This really happened in a bang.

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:47.899
7 minutes into the flight

00:01:47.899 --> 00:01:50.151
and 16,000 feet.

00:01:50.151 --> 00:01:51.194
This plane experienced

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:52.028
what is technically known

00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:53.947
as a rapid decompression

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.156
in the high pressure,

00:01:55.156 --> 00:01:56.616
breathable air inside

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.493
goes rushing outside,

00:01:58.493 --> 00:02:00.286
the cold, thin air outside

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.996
comes rushing inside.

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:03.331
And you heard about that boy

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:04.791
who had his shirt pulled off.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.751
The oxygen mask came down.

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:08.503
The pilots had to deal with this

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:09.462
very quickly.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.214
And I want you to listen to the calm

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:11.840
from the new air

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.424
traffic control recordings we just got.

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:15.135
Step one, fly the airplane.

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.344
Step two, run

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:17.595
the checklist, descend

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:19.347
back down to breathable air.

00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:19.889
You don't have a lot

00:02:19.889 --> 00:02:22.225
of consciousness time in 16,000 feet.

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:23.309
And then communicate

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:28.064
to air traffic control what is going on?

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:31.734
Get out.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:32.694
Number the

00:02:32.694 --> 00:02:35.697
end down at 10,000.

00:02:35.738 --> 00:02:36.573
Possible. Easy.

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:37.073
Roger that.

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:37.448
To maintain

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:39.075
one zero thousand and one April

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:39.450
are going to

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.493
be the nature of the emergency

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:41.494
and your intentions.

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.829
Seattle, Alaska

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:45.748
Public Justice Department.

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:49.669
We think they're getting down to 10,000.

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:50.753
You can hear the pilots

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:51.921
with their muffled voices.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:53.047
Likely they had oxygen

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.966
masks on to the left side

00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:56.467
of the airplane's fuselage

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:58.303
is where this failure occurred.

00:02:58.303 --> 00:02:59.179
There is a door

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:00.722
that can be installed

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:01.723
at the Boeing factory.

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:02.932
It depends on the seating

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:05.226
configuration ordered by the airline.

00:03:05.226 --> 00:03:06.936
This airplane in particular

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:07.979
did not have it.

00:03:07.979 --> 00:03:08.813
Instead,

00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:10.356
it has a plug that you can see

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.192
on the outside of the airplane there.

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:12.817
You wouldn't know about it

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:13.985
from the inside.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.320
This plane in particular

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.197
rolled off the factory floor

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.324
only ten weeks ago

00:03:19.324 --> 00:03:20.366
from Renton, Washington,

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.118
first flight, October 15th,

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:24.120
150 flights for Alaska Airlines

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:24.996
since then.

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:26.039
Boeing now

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.040
under the microscope

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:27.665
once again

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:29.459
after a litany of issues

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:31.294
with the 737 max.

00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:32.921
Two major crashes abroad,

00:03:32.921 --> 00:03:34.756
346 people killed.

00:03:34.756 --> 00:03:36.883
Grounded in the U.S. for 18 months.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.509
And the Boeing has been dogged

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:40.178
by quality control issues since.

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:40.762
The good news

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:43.348
here, 171 passengers on board,

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:45.266
six crew, all okay.

00:03:45.266 --> 00:03:47.560
And the crew did an exceptional job.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.394
Oh, they really did.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:49.938
I mean, that is great news.

00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:50.813
Great news there.

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:52.649
But this is really rattling,

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:53.650
you know, a lot of people.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.734
So any indications

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.069
now that other airlines

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:59.030
might ground their 737 nine max jets?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.616
This is the latest data from Cirium,

00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:03.451
which looks at the global airline fleet.

00:04:03.451 --> 00:04:06.037
And they say there are about 215

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:09.666
737 max nines in use around the world.

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:10.833
One of the biggest operators,

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:12.877
United Airlines, they have 79 of them.

00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:13.962
We've reached out to comment

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:14.963
to see what they will do,

00:04:14.963 --> 00:04:16.339
although we do know now

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.590
that Alaska Airlines,

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.509
all 65 of its planes,

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:21.219
are temporarily grounded,

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.637
pending inspections

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:25.098
of this specific spot.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.557
Alaska Airlines CEO says

00:04:26.557 --> 00:04:28.768
that will take only a few days time,

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:29.394
although we will see

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:30.311
what transpires here.

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:31.938
The National Transportation Safety Board

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:32.563
digging into this

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:33.773
along with the federal Aviation

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:34.357
Administration.

00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:36.109
For an adding to the alarm.

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:39.112
I mean, this is a young aircraft

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:40.738
just ten weeks old.

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:41.114
All right.

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:41.864
Pete Montaigne,

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:42.490
thank you so much

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.241
for checking back with you

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:44.325
as you learn more.

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:45.368
So let's talk

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:46.452
more about the analysis of

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:49.163
this is going to retired 777 Captain

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:50.498
Les Abend.

00:04:50.498 --> 00:04:51.249
Good to see you.

00:04:51.249 --> 00:04:52.542
So what's your reaction,

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.252
your initial reaction to hear that

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:56.087
a piece of a Boeing 737

00:04:56.087 --> 00:04:58.089
nine max plane would simply,

00:04:58.089 --> 00:04:58.464
you know,

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.216
fall off, get sucked away

00:05:00.216 --> 00:05:03.136
and frighten a whole lot of people?

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:05.096
Well, I'm flabbergasted, Fredricka.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.181
I just it's hard for me

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:07.598
to imagine,

00:05:07.598 --> 00:05:08.474
regardless of the fact

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:09.517
that Alaska uses

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:10.643
that or

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:14.314
does or does not utilize that particular

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:16.524
section as a emergency exit,

00:05:16.524 --> 00:05:17.984
that was a service to her.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.652
Pete did a great job of

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:21.946
pretty much describing,

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:23.197
you know what that was all about.

00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:24.449
But at the end of the day,

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:26.200
it's a plug type door is what we call it.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.786
It's like like a bank vault.

00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:30.371
That that door,

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:31.956
if it was used as an emergency

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:32.415
exit,

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:33.791
should have sealed

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.794
tight against that airplane.

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:38.504
How it came out

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.631
is just unfathomable to me.

00:05:40.631 --> 00:05:43.885
But in any case,

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:46.971
the crew did a tremendous job, as we as

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.014
you just talked about.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.350
But the first thing you've got to do

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.434
is take care of yourself

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:52.477
up in the cockpit.

00:05:52.477 --> 00:05:52.977
And that's

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:54.896
and the very first thing

00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:57.523
that we do is we put on our oxygen mask.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:59.817
Now, they were only at 16,000 feet,

00:05:59.817 --> 00:06:02.070
so they had enough oxygen.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.988
It would have got a little dicey

00:06:03.988 --> 00:06:05.406
had they been up there longer,

00:06:05.406 --> 00:06:07.450
but or been at the much higher altitude.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.659
That's the other part of it.

00:06:08.659 --> 00:06:09.911
It's good that it came off

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:11.746
when it did the

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:12.830
and then once you start

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:14.165
with the oxygen mask,

00:06:14.165 --> 00:06:16.125
you start a checklist, like Pete said,

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.252
and you come down very quickly.

00:06:18.252 --> 00:06:19.504
However,

00:06:19.504 --> 00:06:21.381
one of the caveats of coming down

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:22.757
is, is to say, well,

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:24.342
should we come down

00:06:24.342 --> 00:06:26.761
fast or do we have a possible

00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:28.638
structural integrity issue?

00:06:28.638 --> 00:06:29.555
And guess what?

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.516
They probably thought they did.

00:06:31.516 --> 00:06:32.141
Got a call

00:06:32.141 --> 00:06:33.267
from flight attendants and say,

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.268
hey, I think

00:06:34.268 --> 00:06:35.561
the side of the fuselage

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.146
has a big hole in it.

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:39.065
So they probably took a little more time

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:40.108
than they would have.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:43.361
When I looked at the flight aware data,

00:06:43.444 --> 00:06:44.987
they could have come down a lot faster.

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:45.947
But I think they were

00:06:45.947 --> 00:06:48.074
they were using their best judgment.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:49.992
And I and I applaud them for that.

00:06:49.992 --> 00:06:51.786
So in any case,

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:52.912
all the oxygen masks

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:54.664
will automatically come down.

00:06:54.664 --> 00:06:57.750
But it certainly was a very

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.669
harrowing situation

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:01.087
for the passengers back there.

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.381
But the crew did tremendous.

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:05.508
And we celebrate them, thank goodness.

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:07.093
So

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:09.220
when it comes down to the analysis of the

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.639
what happened, you mentioned a seal.

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:11.931
Okay?

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:13.391
This is a space

00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:14.267
where there's an option

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:17.270
for it to be a door or the windows.

00:07:17.353 --> 00:07:18.146
In this case,

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:20.356
you said it was a plug door.

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:22.275
So what kind of seal

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.734
is traditionally around it?

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:24.861
Might that be the area

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:25.778
that's compromised?

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:27.905
Because this isn't a new configuration.

00:07:27.905 --> 00:07:29.240
This is something

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.075
that is, you know, customary

00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:32.618
and on a lot of planes.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:34.328
But something obviously went wrong.

00:07:34.328 --> 00:07:37.665
This is a ten week old aircraft.

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:40.042
What's the first order of business

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:41.294
in trying to figure out

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:44.130
how and why this was compromised?

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.257
Well, if this was a plug type door,

00:07:46.257 --> 00:07:48.259
that could have been used as an option,

00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:49.844
as a service door, which would

00:07:49.844 --> 00:07:52.388
become an emergency exit,

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:52.805
like I

00:07:52.805 --> 00:07:54.515
said, it's a bank vault

00:07:54.515 --> 00:07:56.017
type door situation.

00:07:56.017 --> 00:07:58.269
So having those

00:07:58.269 --> 00:07:58.978
there's pins

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.354
that surround that whole thing.

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:03.191
The seal is just to

00:08:03.191 --> 00:08:06.068
just reinforce all of that.

00:08:06.068 --> 00:08:09.071
And so having that door come open

00:08:09.197 --> 00:08:11.616
with a pressurized airplane,

00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:13.534
it just it's amazing to me.

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:15.620
I don't know how

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.121
that could happen

00:08:17.121 --> 00:08:19.665
unless it wasn't constructed properly.

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:23.503
And if this door was functional

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:25.129
in any particular way,

00:08:25.129 --> 00:08:28.007
the cockpit would have seen a message

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:30.968
that said, Hey, this door is open.

00:08:30.968 --> 00:08:32.053
And so it's wise.

00:08:32.053 --> 00:08:33.012
In your view then, that,

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:33.804
you know, immediately

00:08:33.804 --> 00:08:35.223
these kinds of aircrafts,

00:08:35.223 --> 00:08:36.307
you know, part of any fleet

00:08:36.307 --> 00:08:38.643
would be suspended, you know, grounded

00:08:38.643 --> 00:08:40.603
while they carry out the investigation.

00:08:40.603 --> 00:08:42.396
The NTSB is sending a go team

00:08:42.396 --> 00:08:44.982
to Portland to investigate.

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:47.026
From that standpoint.

00:08:47.109 --> 00:08:48.528
It's an extraordinary

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:49.195
circumstance

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:51.656
and I understand why they're doing it.

00:08:51.656 --> 00:08:52.823
You know,

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:53.950
let's not start talking

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.034
about the history of the max.

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:56.285
No, this certainly isn't related

00:08:56.285 --> 00:08:56.702
to the

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:57.912
to the

00:08:57.912 --> 00:09:00.581
the M care system that we knew back

00:09:00.581 --> 00:09:03.459
in 2019. But,

00:09:03.459 --> 00:09:04.961
you know, like like Pete said,

00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:06.921
there are some quality control

00:09:06.921 --> 00:09:08.631
aspects to this airplane.

00:09:08.631 --> 00:09:10.675
And we're hoping that

00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:11.801
that Boeing gets it right

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:13.970
or this is an anomaly.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.722
You know, this is a one off

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:17.390
situation

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.267
that could have been under human control

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:20.977
that we're not aware of at this point.

00:09:20.977 --> 00:09:22.270
Is it an anomaly

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.188
to have this kind of door

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:25.815
option configuration?

00:09:25.815 --> 00:09:28.025
Is it specific to the 737

00:09:28.025 --> 00:09:29.527
nine max planes,

00:09:29.527 --> 00:09:31.821
or do we need to be concerned about this

00:09:31.821 --> 00:09:32.905
same kind of

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:36.492
option configuration on other aircrafts?

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:39.453
We shouldn't be concerned about this.

00:09:39.453 --> 00:09:41.539
All airlines order their planes

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:43.666
in different types of configurations

00:09:43.666 --> 00:09:44.959
when it comes to seating,

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:47.962
when it comes to a cockpit arrangement.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:49.505
So, you know,

00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:52.508
this is not unusual from that standpoint.

00:09:52.633 --> 00:09:53.009
I guess

00:09:53.009 --> 00:09:54.760
Alaska decided,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:54.969
you know,

00:09:54.969 --> 00:09:57.179
because they have enough emergency exits

00:09:57.179 --> 00:09:58.723
for an evacuation situation

00:09:58.723 --> 00:09:59.765
that they didn't necessarily

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:01.475
have to utilize this door.

00:10:01.475 --> 00:10:03.936
So they utilized it for seating.

00:10:03.936 --> 00:10:04.562
All right.

00:10:04.562 --> 00:10:06.314
Let's say, Ben, thank you so much.

00:10:06.314 --> 00:10:06.689
Of course,

00:10:06.689 --> 00:10:09.191
we are all glad that all passengers,

00:10:09.191 --> 00:10:12.361
all crew are okay.

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:14.864
Pretty shaken up, probably, but okay.

